#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059563 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/id #uuid "8cd94856-fbe5-53a4-b7f2-f5b29b7aa927" :question/math-academy-id "e-4514" :question/problem "Find the inverse of $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -(-4) & -3 \\\\ -(14) & -8 & -(-4) \\\\ 3 & -(-2) & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059564 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/id #uuid "9180ff3b-46d2-5df7-81b6-ac75bef51e00" :question/math-academy-id "e-4515" :question/problem "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059565 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "26e9a55f-7711-5d8f-ab0a-e8638f2d2dec" :question/math-academy-id "e-4516" :question/problem "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7576155e-753f-578e-a4ec-c1e146863345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285876} {:answer/id #uuid "7804ba9f-5287-5069-b31b-fbfc92859355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285906} {:answer/id #uuid "94a9a448-efa8-5e13-8fb2-e9929ad565e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286140} {:answer/id #uuid "e6986340-6e6f-5163-a46c-b7ce43a43001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286898} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4f3922-637f-578e-9c1d-529afc788b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7804ba9f-5287-5069-b31b-fbfc92859355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285906} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f3d5af-0a6c-5449-9acd-f6ef2a68a144" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "882c4c5b-7c6a-5276-989d-a6c4749ae87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18280" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =5|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(0-2)+0+3(-2+3) \\\\  & =-10+0+3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-15-0 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}=\\frac{1}{-7}\\cdot (-15)=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4f2334d-4d19-5657-ae3e-4861480ed63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4f2334d-4d19-5657-ae3e-4861480ed63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298594} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b9513e-697d-5d7b-bee1-be7a3d121ec8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbfc66b-fff8-5004-b6dd-86427e259c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bbfc66b-fff8-5004-b6dd-86427e259c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297674} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0d77a70-66b2-5ffc-82b6-2ce4bec2f8bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299237} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e59730-fbc2-5bbb-83ce-1f84517628d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90e59730-fbc2-5bbb-83ce-1f84517628d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298183} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9818ff9-bf1d-504a-a640-f403039467ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0876fd99-09ab-5402-abb3-c07e19b050b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-319360" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-5.$\n\n|  |  | $-17$ | {{field-1}} | $-1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $12$ | {{field-2}} | $1$ |  |\n|  |  | $-3$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-17 & -12 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 12 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c573351-ee99-56f5-889f-894b79759197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c573351-ee99-56f5-889f-894b79759197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298090} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e78f910-eafd-5dde-95e7-840e8bbf0359" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "959ebdb0-727b-5193-9194-4f73c8f717da" :question/math-academy-id "q-319550" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $-22.$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\,1\\cdot (2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-22}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f85809-939f-5696-b20f-6a24d1b3a8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f85809-939f-5696-b20f-6a24d1b3a8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297553} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c533b51-00bb-5918-948d-af70d14f79e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44f3f3e2-3340-514f-8bf3-b71afd4ac18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-319944" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)((-1)(-1)-(-2)(-2))+2((-2)(-1)-(-2)k)+(-2)((-2)(-2)-(-1)k) \\\\  & =(-2)(1-4)+2(2+2k)+(-2)(4+k) \\\\  & =6+4+4k-8-2k \\\\  & =2k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-((-2)(-2)-(-2)k) \\\\  & =-(4+2k) \\\\  & =-2k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k-4}{2k+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k+2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =2 \\\\ k+2 & =2k+2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
